# Stored definitions this binary cannot parse (spec/design/unparsable-definitions.md).
#
# The Ruby-forged fixture stands in for a file a newer jed wrote: each kind of stored SQL holds text
# no jed parser accepts (`… is future syntax`). Opening it must succeed. Only statements that need an
# unparsed object's parsed form fail, with XX003, and dropping each object works. The tables:
#   t (id, v) rows (1,1) (2,2) (3,-3); views ok (parses), u and r (do not), w (reads u)
#   c (id, v) rows (1,10) (2,20); CHECK c_ok (v > 0) parses, CHECK c_v_check does not
#   d (id, v, k) row (1,1,1); v's expression DEFAULT does not parse, k's is 1 + 1
#   x (id, v) rows (1,1) (2,2); expression index x_future_idx does not parse; x_v_idx on v
#   p (id, v) rows (1,1) (2,2); UNIQUE partial index p_future_idx on v whose predicate does not parse
# The two unparsed indexes are forged EMPTY although their tables have rows, so a planner that used
# either would lose rows. jed-specific, so cross-core-checked, not PostgreSQL-oracle-checked.
# requires: harness.fixture_open, storage.unparsable_definitions, introspect.tables, introspect.views, introspect.columns, introspect.indexes, introspect.constraints, ddl.view, ddl.view_alter, ddl.view_cascade, dml.view_write, ddl.check, ddl.column_default_expr, ddl.index_expr, ddl.index_partial, ddl.alter_table, ddl.drop_table, dml.insert, dml.insert_column_list, dml.update, dml.delete, query.select, query.where_eq, query.order_by, query.join_inner, types.i32, types.text
# fixture: fileformat/fixtures/unparsable_definitions.jed

